ce, not a reaction to more people using it. The system’s current accuracy spec (a few meters) is what it is, and it’s comfortably met with a daily upload cadence, regardless of how many users exist. In practice, more users do not create any feedback loop to the control segment. The ground stations never see your receiver’s data; they only see the satellites’ own brodcasting signals. The monitoring stations are like weather observers measuring a lighthouse’s light output — they don’t know or care how many ships are watching. ### The Real “Scaling” Work Happens in Your Receiver When GPS usage explodes (billions of smartphones), the actual scaling happens at the chipset level, through mass production of ever-cheaper, ever-more-sensitive receivers, and through crowd-sourcing assistance data (like cell networks providing almanac downloads). None of that involves the ground segment working harder. So to sum up: The satellites just shout; the ground control just calibrates the shouting on a fixed, physics-driven schedule; neither knows how many listeners there are. The entire system is beautifully decoupled — that’s exactly why a handful of satellites can serve a planet.
